<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Janggala</h1><p class="st-meta">1045 CE – 1136 CE (approx.) · capital Kahuripan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern of the two kingdoms created when Airlangga divided East Java in 1045, remembered in the Panji romances.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Janggala arose around 1045 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga's</a> partition of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kahuripan">Kahuripan</a>, which gave one son the eastern lands around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas</a> delta, the old capital Kahuripan and the coastal ports near modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-surabaya">Surabaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidoarjo">Sidoarjo</a>. The other son received the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kediri-kingdom">Kediri Kingdom</a>, also called Panjalu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Very few records survive. An inscription of 1052 names King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapanji-garasakan">Mapanji Garasakan</a>, who seems to have fought his western rival. Its coastal position gave it access to sea trade, but documentary evidence for its government is slight.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Janggala was absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> by the early 12th century; an inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayabaya">King Jayabaya</a> of 1135 declares '<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kediri-kingdom">Panjalu</a> jayati', Panjalu is victorious. Its memory lived on in the Panji tales, in which a prince of Janggala searches for his beloved princess of Kediri, stories that spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/janggala.jpg" alt="Janggala" width="320" height="143"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kediri_Kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1045 CE – 1136 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kahuripan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mapanji Garasakan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jenggala, Kingdom of Janggala</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1090&amp;focus=janggala" aria-label="Janggala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7674 2452 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Janggala lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7994 2708L7994 2708M7994 2708L8097 2725"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7994 2708h0M8097 2725h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7994 2708h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7994 2708h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kahuripan. <a href="/atlas?year=1090&amp;focus=janggala">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
